1
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,800
Going into the 1990s,  

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:10,080
running a big beefy enterprise server 
meant UNIX software on RISC hardware.

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:17,440
Then came Intel. And upon its silicon horse rode 
two powerful operating systems. One was Windows,  

4
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:20,720
which everyone first thought would win the market.

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:23,520
But then came this new, fast-moving thing  

6
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:28,240
written with the contributions of 
thousands around the world. Linux.

7
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:33,360
In today's video, one of the most fascinating 
industrial transitions in computer history:  

8
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:38,960
How Intel, Microsoft and Linux 
came together to take down UNIX.

9
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:41,280
## Beginnings

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:48,320
It is the 1990s and Chipzilla is 
seeking new worlds to conquer.

11
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,240
They had won the consumer PC 
world by masterfully leveraging  

12
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:58,080
the PC's massive sales volumes. High 
volume improves semiconductor yield.

13
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:04,320
Better yield reduces costs, raises profits, 
and throws off more cash to invest back into  

14
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:12,240
better manufacturing. Intel grabbed a lead that 
competitors like AMD and Cyrix cannot overcome.

15
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:17,920
Intel believed that it can run the same playbook 
in the world of enterprise servers. Such servers  

16
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:23,040
are used by corporate clients to manage 
and serve multiple “client” computers.

17
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:27,600
Sometimes, people group servers together 
with workstations - which are more powerful  

18
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:34,000
desktop computers for intense tasks like 
graphics or scientific applications. Such  

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
workstations can also serve a file or two 
over a LAN - acting as a low-end server.

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,880
On the high end, there are big monolith 
computers powered by many CPUs with support  

21
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:53,520
for 64-bit and virtual machine as well as lots 
of memory. They run databases, file storage,  

22
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:59,120
and complex business applications - 
providing beefy compute via network.

23
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:04,240
They can also serve websites - which 
later became a massive market as the  

24
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:09,736
Internet craze expanded. Though you 
don't need a high end machine for that.

25
00:02:09,736 --> 00:02:11,200
## Vertically Integrated
At the start of the decade,

26
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:16,800
this enterprise server market was dominated 
by vertically-integrated system vendors.

27
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:23,040
Such vendors offered a closed, proprietary 
system often based on RISC CPUs running a  

28
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:27,920
variant of the UNIX operating system. 
The largest and most famous vendor  

29
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:34,480
was Sun Microsystem - which offered their 
SPARC chips and Solaris operating system.

30
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:40,640
Others included IBM with their POWER RISC 
chips running the AIX operating system.

31
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:45,200
And Hewlett-Packard, with their 
PA-RISC chips running the HP-UX  

32
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:49,040
operating system. Silicon Graphics as well.

33
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:54,960
These systems were expensive - running into the 
hundreds of thousands of dollars. What's more,  

34
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:02,000
their proprietary nature made switching 
nigh impossible. Buy in, get locked in.

35
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:02,080
## Windows NT

36
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:07,840
But if Intel wanted to enter the server 
market, it needed a strong software partner.

37
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:13,200
And where did this bring them? To 
Microsoft ... and Windows NT. Released  

38
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:20,400
in 1993 by a team led by the legendary 
Dave Cutler, NT is a proprietary OS for  

39
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:26,480
both workstations and servers separate 
from the consumer-oriented Windows.

40
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:30,880
While Windows NT Server looks similar 
to the consumer product and can run  

41
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:35,120
the same applications, they were 
quite different beneath the hood.

42
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:42,000
Regular Windows is a graphical shell grotesquely 
grafted on top of the MS-DOS operating system.  

43
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:47,440
It is said to be possible to clip through 
into the horrors of the Backrooms beyond.

44
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:52,640
NT on the other hand was written from the 
ground up for the enterprise. Microsoft  

45
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:58,080
marketed it as being more powerful, 
reliable, and with enhanced security.

46
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:04,320
The first version of NT Server ran on 
the Intel 386. It worked fine but it  

47
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:10,960
didn't exactly set the world ablaze. NT was 
immature, missing features and capabilities  

48
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:19,040
that its competitors - like IBM's OS/2, 
Novell's NetWare and the UNIX variants - had.

49
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:26,560
And it required rather powerful hardware 
(for the time): 16 megabytes of RAM. In 1993,  

50
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:31,760
NT Server sold about 30,000 units 
- undoubtedly a disappointment.

51
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:38,480
But Microsoft is like the Terminator or sequels 
to Air Bud: They just keep on coming. Redmond  

52
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:44,080
stayed in the market - adding new features 
and sending the UNIX vendors scrambling.

53
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:50,880
In 1994, Microsoft sold about 
115,000 NT licenses. An improvement,  

54
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:57,505
though sales still lagged that of even its 
Workstation sibling. But they were growing.

55
00:04:57,505 --> 00:04:57,520
## Pentium Pro

56
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:01,840
In late 1995, Intel presented 
the first chip they really felt  

57
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:06,560
could take them to the server 
space: the 32-bit Pentium Pro.

58
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:08,800
I mentioned the Pentium Pro before.  

59
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:15,360
It had 5.5 million transistors and ran 
at about 150-200 megahertz - a great  

60
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:22,240
leap over the original Pentium's 3.1 
million transistors and 60 megahertz.

61
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:25,600
The Pentium Pro slightly outperformed the fastest  

62
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:31,680
RISC chips in certain integer benchmarks. 
Which took the community by surprise.

63
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:38,720
While faster RISC chips like the MIPS R10000 
quickly matched afterwards, the Pro reset  

64
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:44,880
the perceived performance limits of what was 
previously deemed a mere "desktop" architecture.  

65
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:51,360
The Pentium Pro thusly gave Intel the performance 
wedge it needed to invade the server world.

66
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:57,040
Vendors found that it together with the next 
major version of Windows NT - version 4.0,  

67
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:02,240
released earlier that same year - produced 
a ready-made server standard that can  

68
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:08,960
match the performance of some RISC-based 
integrated systems at a far lower price.

69
00:06:08,960 --> 00:06:16,240
In mid-1995 Intel started circulating example 
boards of what they called "Standard High Volume  

70
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:22,720
Servers". These are basic reference models that 
partners can adopt to accelerate time-to-market.

71
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:27,600
The first generation boards 
can support 1-4 Pentium Pros,  

72
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:33,040
1-2 gigabytes of working memory, and 
hundreds of gigabytes of storage. Intel  

73
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:38,880
said that these servers can handle 
up to 2,000 transactions per second.

74
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:43,040
During the release presentation in 
November, Intel announced Pentium  

75
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:52,000
Pro-powered computers ranging from a 
$2,600 workstation to a $20,000 server.

76
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,840
In 1996, sales of Windows NT 4.0 
grew by 85% - reaching 27% market  

77
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:04,720
share according to IDC. The 
competition remained strong,  

78
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:11,660
but Wintel was building up a strong base in 
servers, like what they have done in consumer.

79
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:11,680
## Border Skirmishes

80
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:17,360
However, Intel knew there were risks in 
chaining its enterprise future to Microsoft.

81
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:22,880
While on the whole Microsoft and Intel 
cooperated to grow the PC ecosystem,  

82
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:29,840
breakouts still happened. Most infamously, in 
1995 Intel introduced a multimedia software  

83
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:34,720
standard they called "native 
signal processing" or NSP.

84
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:40,400
NSP was Intel’s effort to offload some 
demanding audio and video compute to the  

85
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:48,480
CPU via software and APIs. Their thinking was 
simple: Get people to buy faster processors.

86
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:56,000
But Microsoft saw NSP as a "middleware" that 
bypassed the OS and so stepped into Windows’ turf.  

87
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:02,240
Microsoft executives made it extremely clear to 
Intel that they had issues and publicly announced  

88
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:10,320
that they will not support it. CEO Bill Gates sent 
an email to Andy Grove to express his irritation.

89
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:15,280
The NSP Affair was not the only border 
skirmish to occur during this time,  

90
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:19,760
but it is the most well known. Mostly 
because it came out during Microsoft's  

91
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:24,880
anti-trust lawsuit as an example 
of its anti-competitive behavior.

92
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:27,680
And when it came out, the 
two parties pooh-poohed it,  

93
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:34,320
emphasizing that everything was alright. 
But NSP reminded Intel that their partner  

94
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:39,040
was more than willing to exercise its 
leverage to kill things it didn’t like.

95
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:46,080
Unlike the consumer versions of Windows, Windows 
NT was built from the ground-up to be portable.  

96
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:53,680
While most instances of NT ran on x86, it also 
works on other hardwares like DEC's Alpha CPU,  

97
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:58,800
IBM and Apple's PowerPC, and the RISC design MIPS.

98
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:03,360
Microsoft continued to seal more chip 
support deals - skillfully flanking  

99
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:08,960
Intel right in front of it. A thought 
emerged. If NT corners the market,  

100
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:17,187
can Microsoft turn x86 and Intel into just 
another hardware architecture amongst many?

101
00:09:17,187 --> 00:09:17,200
## Mismatches

102
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:21,520
Even if that does not happen, there 
still remained the performance issues.

103
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:26,720
With Moore's Law, Intel gets a new, 
faster, better chip every two years.  

104
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:31,440
But Microsoft puts out a new Windows 
roughly every three to five years.  

105
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:36,720
And that Windows doesn't always use 
everything that Intel's chips had to offer.

106
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:44,640
Most infamously, Intel first released a 
32-bit chip in 1985 - the 386 processor.  

107
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:51,040
Microsoft didn't bring out a 32-bit 
consumer OS until 1995 - Windows 95,  

108
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:54,960
in case you were wondering. Ten years later!

109
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:59,040
Another one occurred during the 
Pentium Pro development process,  

110
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:03,360
recounted by Bob Colwell in his 
book the Pentium Chronicles.

111
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:09,120
Microsoft told Intel in many meetings that 
Windows 95 (then known as "Chicago") was  

112
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:14,480
going to be 32-bit only with all the 
legacy 16-bit code to be removed. This  

113
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:20,080
gave Intel the assurance to optimize 
Pentium Pro for 32-bit performance.

114
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:27,600
So imagine how much constructive confrontation was 
forthcoming when Microsoft publicly announced in  

115
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:36,320
1994 that Chicago's graphics display interface 
- a key piece of the OS - will remain 16-bits.

116
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:40,800
It was too late in the development cycle 
for Intel to change the Pentium Pro,  

117
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:46,400
so the show went on. And then when it came out, 
Intel got dinged in the press for the chip’s  

118
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:53,440
subpar graphics performance on consumer 
desktops. Fortunately not a dealbreaker.

119
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:59,200
And specifically regarding NT and the 
server world, Intel felt that NT 4.0's  

120
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:05,360
limits of 4-8 processors per OS instance 
imposed a performance ceiling that kept it  

121
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:12,800
out of the high end. To compare, some UNIX 
variants can handle up to 64 processors.

122
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:18,880
This mismatch ties back to the two’s different 
growth models, Intel's growth depends on them  

123
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:24,400
selling people the newest chip. So 
they want software that helps do that.

124
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:29,840
Microsoft on the other hand prioritizes 
servicing their whole install base. So  

125
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:36,240
they are going to take it slow - emphasizing 
backwards compatibility and the whole ecosystem.

126
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:41,040
And it is not like they don't invest - 
developing a new Windows involves thousands  

127
00:11:41,040 --> 00:11:46,880
of employees and might cost billions 
of dollars. It's just how software is.

128
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:52,480
So Intel and Microsoft suffer that tension. 
But what choice does Intel have with regards to  

129
00:11:52,480 --> 00:12:00,160
alternatives? In the late 1990s, one very strong 
new candidate appeared on their radar: Linux.

130
00:12:00,160 --> 00:12:00,240
# Stallman

131
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:08,480
In January 1984, a programmer at MIT named Richard 
Stallman formally began a project to replace UNIX.

132
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:11,120
Stallman hailed from the MIT AI Lab,  

133
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:16,560
which appeared in my video about Lisp 
machines, AI, and expert systems.

134
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:21,520
Lisp began as an elegant research 
tool for early AI research. But when  

135
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:27,200
businesspeople recognized that Lisp and 
the AI it powers can be a big industry,  

136
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:33,120
the Lab split apart into two companies, 
Symbolics and Lisp Machines Inc.

137
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:38,000
Symbolics raised millions to try and 
become the Nvidia of its day - hiring  

138
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:44,160
away the Lab's top hackers and leaving it a 
shell of itself. Stallman never forgave this.  

139
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:47,040
"Everything's dying" he would later say.

140
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:50,880
Motivated by deep feelings of 
loss over his hacker community,  

141
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:56,720
he decided to start a new one - founded 
on top of the concept of free software.

142
00:12:56,720 --> 00:13:01,600
Thusly in late 1983, he embarked on 
a project to build a free operating  

143
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:06,240
system that followed UNIX's design 
principles - like its modularity and  

144
00:13:06,240 --> 00:13:11,600
portability across hardware - without 
copying it for trade secret reasons.

145
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:17,360
The thing was called GNU - which he said 
to mean "GNU's Not UNIX". Stallman didn't  

146
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:22,720
know if he would ever finish it, but with such 
things you will never know if you don’t start.

147
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:26,800
Over the next few years, Stallman and his new 
community built out the various bits of the  

148
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:32,720
toolchain. But there was one thing missing: 
The kernel, which suffered incessant delays.

149
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:37,600
This was a pretty major piece. The 
kernel bridges the OS and the hardware.  

150
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:42,080
It manages memory, how much CPU 
time a process gets, disk space,  

151
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:49,342
and more. Even the name sounds important. It sits 
at the very heart of the whole operating system.

152
00:13:49,342 --> 00:13:49,360
## Linus and Linux

153
00:13:49,360 --> 00:13:53,760
That kernel finally arrived thanks to the 
work of a 21-year old Finnish undergraduate  

154
00:13:53,760 --> 00:14:01,360
named Linus Torvalds, who was inspired by 
an educational UNIX-based OS called MINIX.

155
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:05,840
Very famous story. You probably 
heard it before. In August 1991,  

156
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:11,760
he posted on the MINIX Usenet group that he was 
making a free hobbyist OS and requested feature  

157
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:19,280
ideas. It drew contributors from that ecosystem 
and eventually spun off into its own: Linux.

158
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:25,040
Linus's role in the Linux project soon changes 
from coder to that of a leader who reviews  

159
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:30,640
other people’s contributions and merges them 
into the code base. This approach - famously  

160
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:36,720
described as like a bazaar - lets Linux 
ship new code faster than companies can.

161
00:14:36,720 --> 00:14:42,960
The first workable version of Linux 
released in October 1991. Then in 1992,  

162
00:14:42,960 --> 00:14:51,200
version 0.12 adopted Stallman's license, GPL. 
This allowed anyone to read and modify Linux’s  

163
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:57,040
code so long as those modifications 
are also open sourced, if distributed.

164
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:00,720
So GPL prevented companies from taking the code,  

165
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:05,840
adding some salt on top and re-selling 
the result as a competing closed-source  

166
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:12,800
product - thereby saving the Linux kernel from 
the same fragmentation issues that afflicted UNIX.

167
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:18,720
It also assured companies that any work they did 
to improve the kernel would help everyone and not  

168
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:24,480
get locked away. Companies as large as IBM would 
have hundreds of their employees contributing  

169
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:31,897
code. Torvalds would later famously say that 
going GPL was the best thing that he ever did.

170
00:15:31,897 --> 00:15:31,920
## Distros and Red Hat

171
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:36,960
The GPL also made possible the 
business of commercial distribution.

172
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:40,560
Linux is a kernel - a car engine. Very important,  

173
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:45,680
but you still kinda need the rest of the 
car. That might be fine for early adopters,  

174
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:51,040
it is not practical to ask ordinary users 
to build their car before driving it.

175
00:15:51,040 --> 00:15:56,800
So people started doing distributions 
or "distros". Distributions package the  

176
00:15:56,800 --> 00:16:01,520
Linux kernel along with utilities from 
the GNU Project or other free software  

177
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:06,800
to produce an OS bundle that is 
slightly more convenient to use.

178
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:13,520
It is like those mixtapes we senior citizens 
used to pass around to each other in high school.

179
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:18,400
Good distros became popular enough to 
monetize. Someone likened the business  

180
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:23,840
model to selling bottled water. Different 
packaging, same water ... unless you are  

181
00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:30,160
a Nongfu Water extremist and really into 
the "natural over purified" water thing.

182
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:36,240
Anyway. Notable early distributions of this 
GNU/Linux operating system - which I must shorten  

183
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:41,836
to Linux for the sake of brevity but let us not 
forget GNU - include Slackware and Debian ...

184
00:16:41,836 --> 00:16:49,600
... and Red Hat, which was first set up by 
Marc Ewing in 1993. Ewing adopted Linux early,  

185
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:54,000
but after some time realized that he was 
spending more time managing his Linux  

186
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,640
box than actually developing 
software. I know the feeling.

187
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:03,920
So he made a distribution called Red Hat in 
the spare bedroom of his apartment near Duke  

188
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:09,040
University. Named to memorialize a red 
and white striped lacrosse cap he used  

189
00:17:09,040 --> 00:17:15,680
to wear in college. Though the logo looks 
more like a fedora or a trilby, M’lady.

190
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:20,320
Red Hat provided things around Linux 
to make it a more professional product.

191
00:17:20,320 --> 00:17:26,000
Like for example, the Red Hat Package 
Manager utility, which helps users add,  

192
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:31,200
remove, or update software packages 
that do stuff like Web or FTP.

193
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:38,000
The Red Hat distribution can then be put 
on a CD and sold in retail shops. In 1995,  

194
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:43,360
Marc Ewing and colleague Robert Young founded Red 
Hat the company, which earned revenue from selling  

195
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:49,680
those CDs as well as accompanying training, 
education and customer support services.

196
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:54,160
In its first years, Linux was something 
for highly technical hackers and broke  

197
00:17:54,160 --> 00:17:59,040
college students. The rise of Red Hat 
and other commercial distributions like  

198
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:05,654
Caldera helped usher Linux out of that early 
space and into the corporate mainstream.

199
00:18:05,654 --> 00:18:05,680
## An OS With Opportunity

200
00:18:05,680 --> 00:18:10,160
Linux adoption started small 
but grew fast. By mid-1997,  

201
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:15,840
its worldwide user base was conservatively 
estimated at around 3-5 million.

202
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:21,760
Yeah sure that far lags Windows' 100 
million users and Apple Macintosh’s 50+  

203
00:18:21,760 --> 00:18:30,160
million. But in early 1992, Linux had maybe a 
few hundred users. And 1996, maybe 1-2 million.

204
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:35,680
What was driving all this uptake? It was more 
than just price, though price of course played  

205
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:42,240
a factor. A Red Hat disc and box retailed 
at about $50. Windows NT licenses on the  

206
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:48,400
other hand might sell for anywhere from $150 
for a single upgrade to $1100 for a 10-seat  

207
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:56,720
package. And UNIX systems with hardware and 
software will cost $25,000 to $250,000 or more.

208
00:18:56,720 --> 00:19:01,600
Byte Magazine ran a story in 1997 
mentioning a systems consultant for  

209
00:19:01,600 --> 00:19:07,200
Eastman Kodak installing Linux to aid in 
some project for their Photo CD product,  

210
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:12,960
and saving the company $10,000 by not 
getting a high-priced UNIX alternative.

211
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:17,600
Considering how the Photo CD 
ended up, good savings indeed.

212
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:23,440
But interestingly, companies and organizations 
did not adopt Linux just because it was cheap.  

213
00:19:23,440 --> 00:19:28,240
A significant Linux advantage was 
its reliability. One user said that  

214
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:32,720
it was almost "crash-proof", which 
translates to better productivity.

215
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:37,200
And for sophisticated users, they loved that 
they can access its source code - something  

216
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:42,000
Microsoft would never let anyone do with 
Windows. So people can customize their  

217
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,120
Linux to do things on the fly. People 
tweaked Linux to run on a Palm Pilot.  

218
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:54,640
Researchers at Los Alamos used Linux to network 
together 68 PCs to simulate atomic shock waves.

219
00:19:54,640 --> 00:20:00,320
On the other hand, it was true that Linux also 
had glaring shortcomings. First and foremost,  

220
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:05,760
it had nowhere near as many applications 
as Windows or Mac. There was no good Linux  

221
00:20:05,760 --> 00:20:10,960
version of Microsoft Office, Visual 
Basic, or other business software.

222
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:16,320
I like how Byte said all that, then added right 
afterwards that Linux did include lots of tools  

223
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:22,800
that you can use to create your own applications. 
That is the kind of audience, I reckon.

224
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:26,160
Another issue is getting 
support. If something goes wrong,  

225
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:30,800
what documentation can you consult. And 
who can you call? Though that is where  

226
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:36,080
companies like Red Hat and Caldera 
can step in, providing tech support.

227
00:20:36,080 --> 00:20:41,520
One last issue about Linux was its reputation. 
Despite not sharing a direct lineage, it is  

228
00:20:41,520 --> 00:20:49,120
often associated with UNIX - which means receiving 
UNIX's aura of being inscrutable and hard to use.

229
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:53,040
And despite the fast growth, all 
the prestigious names adopting it,  

230
00:20:53,040 --> 00:20:58,320
and the real price-performance benefits, there 
still remained a negative bias amongst company  

231
00:20:58,320 --> 00:21:05,040
executives. A manager might often ask "How can 
you possibly consider it for a critical server?"

232
00:21:06,160 --> 00:21:12,000
Yet even as they ask that, Linux probably was 
already running somewhere in their company.  

233
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:17,200
One anonymous system administrator 
told the SF Chronicle in 1998:

234
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:23,120
> "Many management types distrust Linux because 
it is free. But they put me in charge of running  

235
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:28,760
their servers and I am going to use what 
works best. What works best is Linux".

236
00:21:28,760 --> 00:21:30,400
## Meet Intel

237
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:36,640
In early 1998, Intel started receiving these 
strange requests from across the country.

238
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:39,520
Engineers and researchers at places like US's  

239
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:45,520
Fermi National Lab were asking Intel 
to port its math libraries to Linux.

240
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:51,360
Prior to this, Intel had minimal involvement 
with Linux - maybe fixing a bug or two.  

241
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:56,960
However, checks with OEMs making white-label 
computer servers for customers like small  

242
00:21:56,960 --> 00:22:02,640
Internet Service Providers showed them 
that Linux growth was exploding upwards.

243
00:22:02,640 --> 00:22:08,640
Almost 20% of public web servers ran it - an 
unexpectedly high market share considering  

244
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:14,000
that the OS had little to no presence on 
the desktop. Still doesn't, by the way.

245
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:19,040
This news went straight up to Andy Grove, 
who remarked in rather colorful language  

246
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:23,760
that they should be doing something about 
this. So Intel executives reached out to  

247
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:29,440
Red Hat and in a series of meetings, tried 
to learn about how this company worked.

248
00:22:29,440 --> 00:22:35,600
And it sort of baffled them. Red Hat doesn't 
own the underlying IP behind its core software  

249
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:42,080
product, which anyone can get for free. How 
does that make money? How’s this investable?

250
00:22:42,720 --> 00:22:49,200
But the guys at Red Hat kept talking. CEO 
Robert Young liked to use this ketchup metaphor:  

251
00:22:49,200 --> 00:22:56,080
Anyone with tomatoes, sugar and vinegar can make 
ketchup, yet Heinz owns 80% of that market. And  

252
00:22:56,080 --> 00:23:02,560
Linux advocates inside Intel shared data points 
of what they learned on ISP adoption. In the end,  

253
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:07,680
Intel management was convinced and the two 
companies started talking about partnerships.

254
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:14,960
As I mentioned earlier, Intel increasingly felt 
that it would be risky to rely on just Windows NT.  

255
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:20,960
They were working on a new chip called "Merced", 
the first implementation of their 64-bit IA-64  

256
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:27,440
server architecture. It shall later be branded 
as Itanium, and was said to be blazingly fast.

257
00:23:27,440 --> 00:23:31,600
But worries lingered that NT would 
shackle its full power - same as how  

258
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:36,560
things went with 32-bit for literally a 
decade. Perhaps reinforcing those fears,  

259
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:41,280
Microsoft's next version of NT - 
then called NT 5.0 - was delayed  

260
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:51,040
from 1998 to 1999. After yet further delays, 
Microsoft renamed NT 5.0 to Windows 2000.

261
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:57,360
Now Intel can support and contribute to Linux 
development - tuning it to take advantage  

262
00:23:57,360 --> 00:24:03,680
of Intel's latest and greatest silicon - and 
make it a compelling alternative to Windows.

263
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:03,840
## Summer of Linux

264
00:24:03,840 --> 00:24:09,040
The summer of 1998 saw multiple big 
companies making Linux announcements.

265
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:13,920
In June 1998, IBM - one of the most 
conservative companies out there - said  

266
00:24:13,920 --> 00:24:18,720
that it will include an open source web 
server software called Apache in its suite  

267
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:25,680
of e-commerce tools. Apache isn't Linux, 
but most Apache server instances run on it.

268
00:24:25,680 --> 00:24:30,880
IBM first encountered Apache back in 1996 
when they selected it for the website of  

269
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:35,440
the Atlanta Summer Olympics. Now they 
were giving it their stamp of approval,  

270
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:40,480
officially giving open source its 
first foothold into the enterprise.

271
00:24:40,480 --> 00:24:44,640
Another famous adopter was 
Oracle. On July 6th 1998,  

272
00:24:44,640 --> 00:24:49,840
InfoWorld published an article titled 
"Linux not yet critical mass". In it,  

273
00:24:49,840 --> 00:24:54,560
Oracle - plus Sybase and Informix - said 
they had no intentions of releasing their  

274
00:24:54,560 --> 00:25:00,960
DB software for Linux. A rep said they weren’t 
seeing a big demand from customers for it.

275
00:25:00,960 --> 00:25:04,720
Three days later, Oracle announced 
that they were porting their Oracle8  

276
00:25:04,720 --> 00:25:09,440
database to Linux. A major reason 
for this abrupt turnaround was a  

277
00:25:09,440 --> 00:25:15,760
panel discussion held at Santa Clara in 
July 1998 called "the Future of Linux".

278
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:21,120
Over 900 or so people came to see Torvalds 
and others. In the panel, they discussed  

279
00:25:21,120 --> 00:25:28,640
whether open source was a fad, urged users to push 
application vendors to port to Linux, and more.

280
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:34,800
People later identify that panel as a major 
turning point. By September 1998, several large  

281
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:40,160
companies had Linux ports including the enterprise 
systems company Computer Associates, software  

282
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:46,680
maker Corel, and database company Informix. The 
trend would continue in the subsequent years.

283
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:48,074
## Intel and Red Hat

284
00:25:48,074 --> 00:25:51,360
Intel was also at that July 
"Future of Linux" panel.

285
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:56,320
Intel's Sunil Saxena gave a presentation 
saying that Linux was becoming the OS of  

286
00:25:56,320 --> 00:26:01,360
choice for Internet Service Providers while 
also noting some of its weaknesses - like  

287
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:06,320
scalability, drivers for high end 
hardware, and large memory support.

288
00:26:06,880 --> 00:26:12,080
He emphasized that Intel wanted to support 
Linux. And those words were soon backed with  

289
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:17,440
action on September 29th when Intel announced 
that it - along with the web browser company  

290
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:22,800
Netscape and VC firms Benchmark and 
Greylock - would invest in Red Hat.

291
00:26:22,800 --> 00:26:27,760
Now, Intel was saying they wanted Intel 
silicon to run Linux as well as it did  

292
00:26:27,760 --> 00:26:32,400
Windows NT and everything else. They 
will work with Red Hat - providing  

293
00:26:32,400 --> 00:26:38,880
technical contribution plus a dedicated Intel 
engineering liaison for the Linux community.

294
00:26:38,880 --> 00:26:43,200
Cherry on top, Intel also said they 
will join Linux International - an  

295
00:26:43,200 --> 00:26:47,520
early worldwide Linux advocacy organization.

296
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:52,000
After the announcement, another 
critical but underrated domino falls.  

297
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:57,200
One Benchmark partner was also a board 
member at Dell, and through him Red Hat  

298
00:26:57,200 --> 00:27:02,480
got in front of Michael Dell. They started 
talking about Linux's growth on the server,  

299
00:27:02,480 --> 00:27:07,520
how the market might shape up, and 
if Linux can ever win the desktop.

300
00:27:07,520 --> 00:27:12,160
Dell eventually invested into Red Hat. And 
more importantly, they later announce in  

301
00:27:12,160 --> 00:27:18,640
early 1999 that they will start preinstalling 
Linux on workstations and servers - the first  

302
00:27:18,640 --> 00:27:26,067
major vendor to do this. Suddenly Linux is 
on the same pedestal as Windows and Novell.

303
00:27:26,067 --> 00:27:26,080
## Microsoft

304
00:27:26,080 --> 00:27:30,800
When news of Intel's Red Hat investment 
came out, the tech media went into a  

305
00:27:30,800 --> 00:27:36,720
frenzy speculating what this might mean 
for the Intel-Microsoft relationship.

306
00:27:36,720 --> 00:27:40,480
Red Hat's executives recounted in 
the book "Under the Radar" that  

307
00:27:40,480 --> 00:27:45,280
Microsoft "went ballistic" after Intel 
announced their investment. That weekend,  

308
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:50,240
Intel executives took multiple angry 
calls from their Microsoft counterparts.

309
00:27:50,240 --> 00:27:56,560
Prior to 1998, Microsoft maybe could have nixed 
the Intel investment. But in May that year,  

310
00:27:56,560 --> 00:28:00,720
the US government charged Microsoft 
for monopolistic actions - particularly  

311
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:06,480
regarding how it bundled Internet Explorer 
with Windows during the Browser Wars.

312
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:09,520
The trial and scrutiny meant that Microsoft had to  

313
00:28:09,520 --> 00:28:14,160
temper its behavior. Publicly they 
issued a statement citing Red Hat as  

314
00:28:14,160 --> 00:28:18,960
evidence that Microsoft didn't actually 
have a monopoly on operating systems.

315
00:28:19,760 --> 00:28:23,440
Interestingly, Microsoft would later 
invoke Linux as evidence that there  

316
00:28:23,440 --> 00:28:29,120
remained enough competition in the OS market 
that the government shouldn't break them up.

317
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:33,120
But internally, they were 
concerned. In October 1998,  

318
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:37,680
a set of Microsoft memos - infamously 
named the Halloween Memos - leaked,  

319
00:28:37,680 --> 00:28:43,520
called the rise of free software like Linux 
a long-term credible threat to Windows.

320
00:28:43,520 --> 00:28:47,360
They then reportedly formed a "Linux 
attack team" to convince customers  

321
00:28:47,360 --> 00:28:52,080
that it was not ready. They posted 
a web page called "Linux Myths",  

322
00:28:52,080 --> 00:28:56,320
that tries to "step back from the 
hype and look at the reality".

323
00:28:57,280 --> 00:29:02,400
It then addresses various "myths" chanted 
by the Linux faithful like "Linux performs  

324
00:29:02,400 --> 00:29:09,840
better than Windows NT 4", and "Linux is more 
reliable than Windows NT 4" and "Linux is free".

325
00:29:09,840 --> 00:29:14,800
Infamously in April 1999, Microsoft 
sponsored benchmark tests by a third  

326
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:20,640
party lab called Mindcraft that 
showed NT outperforming Linux  

327
00:29:20,640 --> 00:29:27,262
as a file server by 2.5 times 
and web server by 3.7 times.

328
00:29:27,262 --> 00:29:27,280
## Port of Choice

329
00:29:27,280 --> 00:29:32,080
Intel dodged Gates’ fury by 
declaring that it was OS neutral.

330
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:37,680
So Linux, Novell’s Netware, Sun’s 
Solaris and Microsoft’s Windows 2000,  

331
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:41,280
Intel wanted all of them to 
run well on its hardware.

332
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:45,760
Intel (along with twenty other companies) 
even tried to unite the thirty or so UNIX  

333
00:29:45,760 --> 00:29:49,680
variants out there using a thing 
called Uniform Driver Initiative  

334
00:29:49,680 --> 00:29:55,120
so that vendors can write drivers for 
Intel hardware. It didn't end up so well.

335
00:29:55,120 --> 00:30:00,880
Later in 2010, Intel would formalize this 
Switzerland strategy as "Port of Choice",  

336
00:30:00,880 --> 00:30:07,680
but CTO Pat Gelsinger called it a long-standing 
strategy in 2001. And in the end, the OS  

337
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:13,280
makers had to accept it, because Intel 
silicon then had no real alternatives.

338
00:30:13,280 --> 00:30:19,920
In mid-1998, Intel brought out its follow-up to 
the Pentium Pro: The powerful Pentium II Xeon.  

339
00:30:19,920 --> 00:30:25,600
Intel designed this chip to tackle the mid-range 
workstation and server markets with faster clock  

340
00:30:25,600 --> 00:30:32,480
speeds and a large 512-kilobyte L2 cache that 
made it more suitable for server platforms.

341
00:30:32,480 --> 00:30:38,720
A few months later in March 1999, Intel 
released the Pentium III Xeon - a fast,  

342
00:30:38,720 --> 00:30:44,960
cheap CPU for web and e-commerce transactions. 
While the speed boost over Pentium II Xeon was  

343
00:30:44,960 --> 00:30:51,840
small, the successor did feature support for 
8-way systems via something called Profusion.

344
00:30:51,840 --> 00:30:56,960
This means vendors can build servers with 
eight symmetrical processors - essentially  

345
00:30:56,960 --> 00:31:02,240
gluing eight CPUs together inside 
one box. This broke through a prior  

346
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:08,080
4-way system ceiling that was then causing 
performance issues in existing data centers.

347
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:12,880
To note, this 4-way and 8-way systems 
feature did require the OS to support  

348
00:31:12,880 --> 00:31:20,240
it. This was something that Windows NT/2000 
can do, and Linux then had issues with.

349
00:31:20,240 --> 00:31:24,000
High end UNIX servers still 
retained key advantages. Namely,  

350
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:28,480
certain software like large-scale 
databases, data warehouses and CAD  

351
00:31:28,480 --> 00:31:34,800
systems required far more main memory than 
what x86's 32-bit architecture can allow.

352
00:31:35,360 --> 00:31:37,760
But thanks to this pair of Pentiums,  

353
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:44,378
the low to middle ground of UNIX servers 
found its flanks open to massive competition.

354
00:31:44,378 --> 00:31:44,400
## The Web Scales Out

355
00:31:44,400 --> 00:31:49,760
Sun initially welcomed the Linux revolution. It 
was in line with their motto of being "open".

356
00:31:49,760 --> 00:31:54,560
So they ported their Java Development Kit 
to Linux, and released a Linux port of  

357
00:31:54,560 --> 00:31:58,880
workstation hardware. The general 
impression they gave was that Sun  

358
00:31:58,880 --> 00:32:04,800
doesn't care whether a box runs Linux or 
Solaris - it just has to be a SPARC box.

359
00:32:04,800 --> 00:32:10,560
But that turned out to be a problem. It didn't 
have to be a SPARC box. Serving websites,  

360
00:32:10,560 --> 00:32:16,560
email hosting, firewall and other stuff are 
not like running DB or analytics software.

361
00:32:16,560 --> 00:32:20,960
Companies realized that scaling out 
with nodes of Intel silicon running  

362
00:32:20,960 --> 00:32:27,280
Linux made more financial sense than 
scaling up a big honking UNIX server.

363
00:32:27,280 --> 00:32:32,640
This trend suddenly accelerated after the 
Dotcom stock bubble exploded. Venture money  

364
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:37,680
dried up and startups started dying. With 
frugality the word of the day, companies  

365
00:32:37,680 --> 00:32:43,840
saved money by buying second-hand equipment 
bought from liquidated failures or Linux.

366
00:32:43,840 --> 00:32:47,520
Amazon did a major Linux transition in 2001,  

367
00:32:47,520 --> 00:32:51,840
mentioning in their quarterly SEC filing 
that it cut their costs by an estimated  

368
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:56,960
$17 million per quarter. When asked about 
what they did, an Amazon spokesman said:

369
00:32:56,960 --> 00:33:02,560
> "We've always been pretty closed-mouthed about 
technology on the back-end stuff, partly because  

370
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:07,840
it's pretty steep tuition and we don't want 
other people going to school on our tuition"

371
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:16,320
The online broker Etrade published a fascinating 
2006 case study. It compared a Sun 4500 Enterprise  

372
00:33:16,320 --> 00:33:25,680
Server that costs $250,000 and can serve about 300 
to 400 users to a Linux-Intel box that costs just  

373
00:33:25,680 --> 00:33:33,200
$4,000 but serves only 180 people. 
How to fix the gap? Get two boxes.

374
00:33:33,200 --> 00:33:39,120
Starting in 2001, Linux boxes with Intel silicon 
got good enough to steadily replace Sun's in  

375
00:33:39,120 --> 00:33:45,600
everything from airline fare to commodities 
contracts trading to financial calculations.

376
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:52,240
Sun tried their own scale-out web play with 
the Netra. The 2001 Netra X1 model cost as  

377
00:33:52,240 --> 00:33:58,640
little as $1,000, but commodity x86 still 
provided better performance for the dollar.  

378
00:33:58,640 --> 00:34:01,040
The company never really formulated a proper  

379
00:34:01,040 --> 00:34:07,503
response to this transition from 
Solaris on SPARC to Linux on Intel.

380
00:34:07,503 --> 00:34:07,520
## Going Upscale

381
00:34:07,520 --> 00:34:12,160
After winning the low-end game, Linux 
now turned its sights to scaling up.

382
00:34:12,160 --> 00:34:17,120
In late 2000, IBM's CEO Lou Gerstner 
- believing that Linux will change the  

383
00:34:17,120 --> 00:34:21,440
world in the same way as the Internet 
- announced a massive Linux effort,  

384
00:34:21,440 --> 00:34:26,080
investing $1 billion and 
dedicating 1,500 employees.

385
00:34:26,080 --> 00:34:31,520
January 2001 saw the release of Linux 
2.4. Red Hat quickly adopted it for a  

386
00:34:31,520 --> 00:34:37,360
subsequent release. It arrived a year 
late, but Linux 2.4 delivered the goods  

387
00:34:37,360 --> 00:34:43,920
in terms of closing some major performance 
gaps with high-end UNIX servers and NT.

388
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:48,480
A major flashpoint during the 1999 
Mindcraft tests a few years prior  

389
00:34:48,480 --> 00:34:54,160
involved Linux's support of multiprocessor 
boxes. That test - confirmed in subsequent  

390
00:34:54,160 --> 00:35:01,760
tests - showed that Linux 2.2 did not scale 
very well beyond two CPUs in a single system.

391
00:35:01,760 --> 00:35:06,640
Windows NT 4.0 and 2000 if 
you recall did have that 4,  

392
00:35:06,640 --> 00:35:12,720
8, and even 32-way processor support. 
So not having it was a major weakness.

393
00:35:12,720 --> 00:35:17,360
So Torvalds and company rolled up 
their sleeves and worked on it. 2.4  

394
00:35:17,360 --> 00:35:23,280
addressed issues like spin locks - allowing 
Linux to use 16 or even 32 processors with some  

395
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:29,440
effectiveness. Though I’m not sure how well 
it actually ran with that high of a number.

396
00:35:29,440 --> 00:35:33,920
It didn't make Linux a challenger to 
high end UNIX machines overnight. But  

397
00:35:33,920 --> 00:35:39,840
the ability to capably support at least 4 
or 8 CPUs cleared a path for Linux on Intel,  

398
00:35:40,720 --> 00:35:45,120
or Lintel, to surge into the 
middle ground of compute tasks.

399
00:35:45,120 --> 00:35:49,200
The economics of Lintel suddenly got 
very compelling. A Dell server with  

400
00:35:49,200 --> 00:35:53,120
four Xeons running Red Hat Linux at $24,000 costs  

401
00:35:53,120 --> 00:35:58,800
half as much as a comparable Sun Fire 
UNIX box but performs just as well.

402
00:35:58,800 --> 00:36:04,720
Red Hat and IBM immediately began targeting 
Sun's Solaris customer base. Hewlett-Packard  

403
00:36:04,720 --> 00:36:09,760
and SGI started offering their customers 
running proprietary hardware the option to  

404
00:36:09,760 --> 00:36:16,480
move to a Linux solution - reasoning that it is 
better to maintain that customer relationship.

405
00:36:16,480 --> 00:36:20,640
2002 was a hard year for the server 
market, but Linux boxes still grew  

406
00:36:20,640 --> 00:36:26,240
swimmingly at the expense of RISC based 
systems. Gartner reported that in 2002,  

407
00:36:26,240 --> 00:36:32,640
Linux server hardware revenue grew 63% 
while UNIX revenue hardware fell 11%.

408
00:36:33,280 --> 00:36:39,440
UNIX hardware was still larger revenue-wise 
- $17 billion in 2002 compared to Linux's $2  

409
00:36:39,440 --> 00:36:43,840
billion - just because these are high end 
machines that cost more. But the pressure  

410
00:36:43,840 --> 00:36:56,480
was there. By 2004, the average selling price of a 
Sun server had fallen 31% from $22,920 to $15,860.

411
00:36:56,480 --> 00:36:57,200
## Opteron and x86-64

412
00:36:57,200 --> 00:37:01,840
One major effort that Intel wanted 
for Linux early on was 64-bit support,  

413
00:37:01,840 --> 00:37:05,040
meaning for IA-64 or Itanium.

414
00:37:05,040 --> 00:37:11,200
In April 1999, Intel spun up Project Trillian, a 
consortium of companies that would confidentially  

415
00:37:11,200 --> 00:37:16,720
receive Itanium prototypes so they can 
develop a Linux port. And in February 2000,  

416
00:37:16,720 --> 00:37:21,920
the project released the IA-64 Linux 
source code early to the community.

417
00:37:21,920 --> 00:37:27,120
Trillian was a milestone in open source 
software development history. That being said,  

418
00:37:27,120 --> 00:37:31,840
Itanium didn't end up replacing 
the 32-bit version of x86, though  

419
00:37:31,840 --> 00:37:37,440
HP did adopt it to replace the processors 
using their proprietary flavors of UNIX.

420
00:37:37,440 --> 00:37:42,960
As we all know, it would be AMD not Intel 
that kicked off the 64-bit transition  

421
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:49,680
with their groundbreaking Opteron series of 
chips in mid-2003. Some time later in 2004,  

422
00:37:49,680 --> 00:37:57,440
Intel released the Nocona Xeon, adopting 
AMD's backwards compatible 64-bit extension.

423
00:37:57,440 --> 00:38:02,000
64-bit had been one of the last things 
that higher-end UNIX systems still had  

424
00:38:02,000 --> 00:38:08,640
over x86. A 32-bit x86 computer addresses 
about 4 gigabytes of RAM - far less than  

425
00:38:08,640 --> 00:38:14,720
what high end applications like databases or 
scientific simulations need to hold in memory.

426
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:20,320
After going 64-bit, an x86 system can now 
address dozens or even hundreds of gigabytes of  

427
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:26,560
RAM - removing one of the last major architectural 
barriers from switching away from UNIX.

428
00:38:26,560 --> 00:38:31,760
Some initially felt that the way Intel 
adopted 64-bit was a bit of a kludge or  

429
00:38:31,760 --> 00:38:38,880
only a stopgap towards Itanium. Eh, 
the results are undeniable. x86-64  

430
00:38:38,880 --> 00:38:45,669
bludgeoned the last walls surrounding UNIX. 
The Lintel barbarians were now in the castle.

431
00:38:45,669 --> 00:38:45,680
## Windows

432
00:38:45,680 --> 00:38:52,880
Then in late 2003, you had the release of Linux 
2.6 - which turned out to be a very iconic kernel.

433
00:38:52,880 --> 00:38:58,240
Everything about it was improved - speed, 
networking, support for up to 64 processors,  

434
00:38:58,240 --> 00:39:03,280
and more. You might consider it the 
first "enterprise-ready" Linux kernel.

435
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:09,120
Moreover, it arrived just as users of the 
now-antiquated Windows NT 4.0 began their  

436
00:39:09,120 --> 00:39:14,160
upgrade cycles. They were single and 
ready to mingle. IBM, Hewlett-Packard,  

437
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:19,840
Dell and the other sirens sang their 
lungs out to woo customers to Linux.

438
00:39:19,840 --> 00:39:24,640
IBM in particular led the charge - 
training thousands of people in Linux,  

439
00:39:24,640 --> 00:39:28,720
helping developers rewrite their 
apps for Linux and providing massive  

440
00:39:28,720 --> 00:39:33,040
marketing dollars and discounted 
machines to potential switchers.

441
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:37,840
How do you fight something that anyone can 
download for free?Microsoft's general pitch  

442
00:39:37,840 --> 00:39:42,320
was that everyone is already using Office, 
so it would be expensive and troublesome  

443
00:39:42,320 --> 00:39:47,200
to switch to Linux than it would be 
to just buy Windows Server licenses.

444
00:39:47,200 --> 00:39:52,240
In 2004, Microsoft ran a "Get the facts" 
marketing campaign sharing research purporting  

445
00:39:52,240 --> 00:39:57,840
to show Windows in a better light compared 
to Linux. The website collected controversy  

446
00:39:57,840 --> 00:40:03,120
as those studies were accused as not being 
very "impartial" and potentially misleading.

447
00:40:03,680 --> 00:40:08,000
A method that I think worked far better 
was the deep lock-in afforded by a product  

448
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:12,080
called Active Directory - which Ben 
Thompson of Stratechery has since  

449
00:40:12,080 --> 00:40:17,280
called "one of the most valuable free 
products of all time". What is that?

450
00:40:17,280 --> 00:40:21,920
Released as part of Windows 2000, 
Active Directory authenticates and  

451
00:40:21,920 --> 00:40:27,440
authorizes all the users and assets inside 
a Windows network. It lets you push updates,  

452
00:40:27,440 --> 00:40:32,720
assign security rights to groups per the 
org chart, and validates authentication.

453
00:40:32,720 --> 00:40:38,640
So with AD, an administrator can onboard a 
new manager, verify their credentials and  

454
00:40:38,640 --> 00:40:44,080
give them access to the right tools. And users 
can log in to various things with one set of  

455
00:40:44,080 --> 00:40:51,280
credentials. It turns a bunch of random Windows 
machines into a single manage-able organization.

456
00:40:51,280 --> 00:40:55,360
As Ben points out, it also gave 
Microsoft ownership of individual  

457
00:40:55,360 --> 00:40:59,840
identity. And since identity reached 
so deeply into the organization,  

458
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:05,120
it raised the cost of switching to a 
level far beyond "just" an OS swap.

459
00:41:05,120 --> 00:41:09,440
So in defiance of the people predicting 
that Linux would take down Microsoft,  

460
00:41:09,440 --> 00:41:12,880
Windows Server sales kept growing. In 2005,  

461
00:41:12,880 --> 00:41:18,560
Windows matched UNIX revenue for the first time 
ever. The next year, they would surpass it.

462
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:23,760
Linux kept growing, but that growth was 
less from eating Windows and more from  

463
00:41:23,760 --> 00:41:30,080
eating UNIX or growing in brand new markets where 
Windows never got a foothold - supercomputing,  

464
00:41:30,080 --> 00:41:35,506
high performance compute, 
web, and in the 2010s, cloud.

465
00:41:35,506 --> 00:41:37,680
## Conclusion
UNIX server sales would slowly decline in

466
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:40,960
the following years. But it 
has not completely died out!

467
00:41:40,960 --> 00:41:46,560
Even today, there remain some 
customers on Solaris, HP-UX or AIX.  

468
00:41:46,560 --> 00:41:50,480
Many such laggard applications 
- usually centered in banking or  

469
00:41:50,480 --> 00:41:55,840
healthcare - are for one reason or another 
too challenging or risky to port to Linux.

470
00:41:55,840 --> 00:42:00,400
So why did UNIX lose? I might propose 
that it had less to do with technology  

471
00:42:00,400 --> 00:42:04,960
than business model. These vertically 
integrated kingdoms worked fine years  

472
00:42:04,960 --> 00:42:09,760
ago until Intel arrived with 
its high volume economics.

473
00:42:09,760 --> 00:42:16,000
First, it was Windows NT who hit UNIX hard. 
But Linux would go at them even harder - being  

474
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:22,480
able to offer customers everything that UNIX had 
without the lock-in. Once Linux got good enough,  

475
00:42:22,480 --> 00:42:27,200
the UNIX vendors had no choice 
but to make the switch themselves.

